Moth uses `Int` for whole numbers and `Float` for decimal numbers.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    lives Int = 3
    progress Float = 0.75
]

An `Int` can be used where a `Float` is expected:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    lives Int = 3
    display_value Float = lives
]

A `Float` never silently becomes an `Int`. That conversion can lose
information, so it needs an explicit cast.

### The wider numeric family

The accepted design also includes fixed-width integers (`I8`, `I16`, `I32`,
`I64`, `U8`, `U16`, `U32`, `U64`), fixed-precision binary floats (`F16`, `F32`,
`F64`), the one-byte octet type `Byte` and the exact `Number` family (`Number`,
`Number0` and `Number1` through `Number256`).

`Int` and `Float` stay separate types. Neither is a shorthand for a fixed-width
type, even when the selected width matches, because one compiler-owned numeric
profile chooses the `Int` width and the `Float` precision independently. The
default profile is `Int32` with `Float64`.

`Byte` is not a number. It holds one octet, compares as an unsigned byte value
and needs an explicit conversion to `U8` before any arithmetic. A `{Byte}` is an
ordinary collection rather than a special buffer or text type.

**Current versus accepted.** The Alpha compiler supports all four `Int`/`Float`
runtime profiles, destination-aware fixed-width and `Byte` literals, typed
fixed-width operators and casts, and folded numeric operations. HTML-JS
executes fixed-width and `Byte` values; experimental HTML-Wasm executes fixed
integers, `Byte`, `F32` and `F64` with checked trap-mode arithmetic, infallible
numeric expression casts that do not involve `F16`, plus `Byte -> U8` and
`U8 -> Byte`. It also supports infallible integer-to-`String` expression casts
from profile `Int` and fixed signed/unsigned integer types. Remaining builtin
runtime casts stay gated, including fallible casts, conversions involving
`Bool`, `Char` or `Error` (including `Char -> Int`), parsing from `String`,
`Float`-family formatting to `String`, and statement-form casts. Recoverable
numeric errors remain gated. The `Number`/`NumberN`
family is not implemented. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.
